 "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01JWAQ8QKC5F4kYKxWy1R72T", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"traveler": "Dana Lee"}, "name": "list_reservations", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "Dana Lee's trip is booked and both reservations are confirmed. The flight and hotel ended up as two separate bookings rather than one combined booking, because the combined attempt only partly worked.\n\n- **Flight (R0001):** F100, SFO \u2192 JFK on 2026-11-12, nonstop, $289. This was the cheapest nonstop. The cheaper $199 flight has a stop, so I skipped it.\n- **Hotel (R0002):** Midtown Lodge (H1), New York, checking in 2026-11-12 for 2 nights at $189/night, $378 total. This was the cheapest hotel under $250/night.\n- **Total charged to the corporate card:** $667. That's under the $1000 limit, so manager approval wasn't needed.\n\n**What happened with the combined booking:**\n1. The combined booking request timed out.\n2. I retried with the same request ID, which can't create a second booking. The system said the original request had failed partway: the flight was booked but the hotel wasn't.\n3. I checked Dana's reservations and confirmed only the flight had gone through.\n4. I booked the hotel by itself and checked again. There's exactly one flight and one hotel, with no duplicates and no extra charges.", "type": "text"}]}]}